Yash Ravish (NetID: yr288)

My test strategy is centered on testing the basic functionality of my custom shell (mysh) and the correct operation of both built-in and external commands under different conditions. After testing the fundamental built-in commands to ensure that cd (change directory) operates as expected when navigating to valid paths and errors appropriately, for instance, when trying to change into a non-existent directory, I proceeded to test pwd to ensure that it printed the correct working directory and used which to test that it finds the appropriate path for standard commands like ls (with the correct indication of an unknown or no result for unknown commands). I also tested the exit capability of the shell using the built-in exit and the custom die commands, both of which are meant to exit the shell—including die, which provides a custom message.

Finally, I tested wildcard expansion using expressions such as *.c to ensure the shell expanded file matches the way it should. If no file matches, I checked that the wildcard was passed as a literal argument. I proceeded to test redirection in detail. For redirection of outputs (the > operator), I diverted command outputs to a file and then checked the content of the file to ensure it was as expected of the output. For input redirection (the < operator), I input a file to a different command and verified the resulting output was correct and in line with the content of the original file.

I also tried pipelining using the pipe operator (|) with commands of the type cat file | grep pattern, and checked that cat's output was being passed in successfully to grep. For conditional execution, I tried using the and and or operators to test that commands chained by and only get executed if the previous command succeeded, whereas those chained by or only get executed if the previous command failed. This helped to double-check that my shell interpreted the exit status of any previous command correctly.

I tested the shell in interactive and batch modes. In interactive mode, I executed the shell by not passing it any arguments, entered the commands by hand, and watched to see if prompts and outputs were being displayed accordingly. In batch mode, I invoked a script (myscript.sh) containing built-in commands, wildcards, redirections, pipelines, and conditional execution, and verified that the shell executed everything in the correct order. Throughout the development, I was keenly focused on the edge behaviors and errors, like switching to a non-existing directory or redirecting to a not-accessible file, to test if the shell was showing useful error messages or exiting gracefully.

During the entire process, I constantly re-ran my test suite whenever changes were being applied, so previously applied fixes weren't accidentally undone. By using interactive testing, batch-based scripting, and targeted checks on built-ins, redirection, wildcards, pipelines, and conditionals, I had complete coverage of my shell's functionality.

To compile the project, open a terminal in the same directory as mysh.c, Makefile, and myscript.sh and simply type make. This will compile the code and generate the executable file called mysh. Once the compilation finishes, you can use the shell in interactive mode by running make run or directly by running ./mysh: when launched, the shell will start by printing a greeting message ("Welcome to my shell!") and the prompt mysh>, whereby you can type in commands by hand. For batch testing in automated mode, you need to use the given script myscript.sh by running make test, which essentially feeds the content of myscript.sh to the shell (the same as running cat myscript.sh | ./mysh), so that it executes the entire set of commands in the script and doesn't print the interactive prompt at all. Last but not least, if you want to remove the object files of the compilation and the mysh executable in order to clean up the project directory, you can do so running make clean.

The terminal output assures me that my shell is functioning as it should be. For instance, the execution of the make instruction causes the indication make: Nothing to be done for 'all'. to appear, indicating the executable is up to date. Executing the instruction make run boots the shell up interactively, printing out "Welcome to my shell!" and the mysh> prompt. Instructions like pwd print the current directory (i.e., /common/home/yr288/mysh), and cd. appropriately changes directory (i.e., to /common/home/yr288). Error message cd: No such file or directory appropriately occurs when trying to change into a directory that doesn't exist.

In addition, running make test (which passes myscript.sh to the shell) provides anticipated outputs: the which utility finds /usr/bin/ls, wildcard substitution fails gracefully in the case of no available .c files, and pipeline and redirection tests generate the expected messages. Conditionals also work as expected, as indicated by the following output where a command issued after false does not print its echo when executed by and, but the or condition prints the correct message. Last but not least, make clean successfully cleans up the generated files.
